\description{
Uses \code{rstan} with pre-compiled Stan code, or \code{cmdstan}, to get posterior draws of parameters from a Bayesian ordinal (or binary) cumulative probability regression model, with a choice of 5 link families -- \code{borm()} is to \code{\link[=blrm]{blrm()}} as \code{\link[rms:orm]{rms::orm()}} is to \code{\link[rms:lrm]{rms::lrm()}}: it generalizes the single (logistic) link that \code{blrm()} implements to all of the link families \code{orm()} supports, and is designed, like \code{orm()}, to scale to a very large number of distinct Y values.  Unlike \code{blrm()}, \code{borm()} does not implement the partial proportional odds model (\code{ppo}/\code{cppo}) itself; \code{blrm()} remains the function to use for that. Instead \code{borm()} implements \code{ydep}, a more general Y-dependent effect generalization that plays \code{cppo}'s role: \code{yde} (paralleling \code{ppo}) identifies which predictors get a Y-dependent effect, and \code{ytrans} (paralleling \code{cppo}, but returning a matrix of one or more columns instead of a single score) specifies the shape(s) of that dependency. A single-column \code{ytrans} with a single \code{yde} predictor reduces mathematically to \code{blrm()}'s own \code{cppo} mechanism.
}
\details{
The Stan code internally uses a Dirichlet prior on the multinomial (category) probabilities at the predictor means, inducing a prior for the intercepts, exactly as \code{\link[=blrm]{blrm()}}'s own default (\code{iprior=0}) does -- \code{borm()} does not offer \code{blrm()}'s other two intercept-prior choices, since simulations showed they slow sampling badly once the number of distinct Y values is large, the regime \code{borm()} is specifically meant for.  Likewise, no data-dependent conditionals appear in the Stan code at all: censoring is handled entirely through subscripts precomputed once in R by \code{\link[rms:ormIdx]{rms::ormIdx()}} (the same routine \code{\link[rms:orm.fit]{rms::orm.fit()}} uses), not through per-observation branching in Stan.

Design matrix columns are centered and QR-orthogonalized (via \code{rmsb}'s internal \code{selectedQr()}, the identical mechanism \code{\link[=blrm]{blrm()}} uses) before being handed to the Stan code, so that highly collinear columns do not hinder posterior sampling; \strong{this transformation is done entirely in R, not in the Stan code}, which receives and fits \code{beta} on the already-transformed scale and knows nothing of the original covariate scale. Parameters are transformed back to the original data scale before being returned. Stan diagnostic output (e.g. from \code{\link[=stanDx]{stanDx()}} or \code{\link[=stanGet]{stanGet()}}) will show intercepts and coefficients on the centered/QR-transformed scale; everything \code{borm()} itself returns and prints is on the original, uncentered scale.

As with \code{\link[=blrm]{blrm()}}, priors on regression coefficients (\code{beta}) are specified through contrasts, via \code{pcontrast}, rather than directly; see \code{\link[=blrm]{blrm()}}'s own documentation for the full \code{pcontrast} syntax, which \code{borm()} reuses unchanged (contrasts on \code{beta} alone). \code{tau} (the \code{ydep} coefficient matrix, present only when \code{yde}/\code{ytrans} are given) gets a single default prior, \code{normal(0, tausd)} independently on every entry, which \code{npcontrast} can override/augment for specific contrasts -- but unlike \code{blrm()}'s own \code{npcontrast} (which can only reference \code{tau}), \code{borm()}'s \code{npcontrast} can reference \code{beta}, \code{tau}, or both in the same contrast, since a Y-dependent effect is a combination of the two (\verb{beta_j + Z_j . tau . ytransmat[y,]}, the effect of predictor \code{j} at Y value \code{y}). Each named predictor-setting list in \code{npcontrast} may include \code{ycut}, the Y value at which to evaluate that setting's \code{tau} contribution (see \code{pcontrast}'s own documentation for the general named-setting-list/\code{contrast=expression()} syntax, which is otherwise identical); a setting with no \code{ycut} contributes a pure-\code{beta} row, letting simple contrasts reduce to exactly the pure-\code{beta} or pure-\code{tau} case with no special syntax -- e.g. two settings sharing the same covariate values but different \code{ycut} have their \code{beta} parts cancel exactly in a difference, giving a contrast purely on the Y-dependent effect.

\code{borm()} implements a single-level hierarchical random intercept for repeated-measures/clustered data via \code{cluster()} in the model formula, exactly as \code{\link[=blrm]{blrm()}} does, and additionally implements the dual-sigma \code{mix_re} extension to that random intercept (from \code{orm.rfit()}, \code{rms}'s internal, undocumented random-intercept fitter) via a \code{mix_re()} term in the formula (used together with \code{cluster()}), exactly as \code{\link[rms:orm]{rms::orm()}} does -- see \code{\link[rms:orm.fit]{rms::orm.fit()}}'s own documentation, under \code{mre}, for the full statistical description of this extension. \code{mix_re} is not a \code{borm()} argument; it is extracted from the formula the same way \code{cluster} is.

When using the \code{cmdstan} backend, \code{cmdstanr} will need to compile the Stan code once per computer, only recompiling when the Stan source code changes. By default the compiled code is stored in directory \code{.rmsb} under your home directory. Specify \code{options(rmsbdir=)} to specify a different location.

If you want to run MCMC sampling even when no inputs or Stan code have changed, i.e., to use a different random number seed for the sampling process when you did not specify \code{sampling.args(seed=...)}, remove the \code{file} before running \code{borm}.

Set \code{options(rmsbmsg=FALSE)} to suppress certain information messages.
}
